## The model doesn't choose

A language model's output isn't a word. It's a <GlossaryPopover term="distribution">probability distribution</GlossaryPopover>: a chance for every
possible next piece of text, all adding up to 100%. Choosing the word is a separate step, called <GlossaryPopover term="sampling">sampling</GlossaryPopover>, and it has its own settings.

Below are GPT-2's ten most likely next words after *The cat sat on the*. Spin the wheel, then move
the sliders and watch the bars change.

<TemperatureSampler />

*Simplified:* to keep the picture readable, the widget only uses GPT-2's ten most likely next words
and rescales them to add up to 100%. The real model chooses from all 50,257 options.

## Temperature

<At levels="simple">

The simplest way to choose is to always take the most likely word. That gives the same text every
time, and with big models it tends to be dull and to repeat itself.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

**Temperature** controls how adventurous the pick is. A low temperature makes the likely words even
likelier. A high one evens out the chances, so unusual words get picked more often. At 0, the
model always takes the top word.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The simplest choice is to always take the most likely word. That's called greedy decoding, and it
gives the same output every time. With large models, always picking the top word tends to produce
dull text that can get stuck repeating itself.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

**Temperature** controls how adventurous the pick is. Below 1, it makes the likely words even
likelier. Above 1, it evens the chances out, so unusual words get picked more often. At 0 it becomes
greedy: always the top word.

</At>

<At levels="standard deep">

Under the hood, the model gives every word a raw score, and the scores are divided by the
temperature before they become probabilities. Try it:

<TemperatureMath />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Temperature, written down

Each candidate token $i$ gets a raw score $z_i$, called a logit. Softmax turns the scores into
probabilities after dividing each one by the temperature $T$:

$$
p_i = \frac{e^{z_i / T}}{\sum_{j} e^{z_j / T}}
$$

where $e$ is the constant 2.718… and the sum runs over all 50,257 tokens, so the $p_i$ add up
to 1. For any two tokens, $p_i / p_j = e^{(z_i - z_j) / T}$: a gap in scores counts for more at a low
$T$ and for less at a high one. At $T = 1$ the model's own probabilities come out unchanged. As $T$
gets close to 0, all the probability goes to the top score, which is why 0 means greedy. Adding the
same number to every score changes nothing; only the gaps matter.

The recorded runs further down divide GPT-2's raw scores by $T$ and draw from all 50,257 tokens;
the widget's cut to ten is only for the picture. Two other controls, next, cut off the long tail
of unlikely tokens instead of flattening it. In the widget they apply after the temperature, top-k
first, and what's left is rescaled to add up to 100%.

</At>

## Top-k and top-p

**Top-k** is a simpler control: only the *k* most likely words are allowed, and the rest are set to
zero.<Cite id="fan2018" /> It stops the model from ever picking something very unlikely, which is
where nonsense tends to come from.

**Top-p**, also called nucleus sampling, cuts by chance instead of by count: it keeps the most
likely words until their chances add up to *p*, and drops the rest.<Cite id="holtzman2020" /> So it
keeps a few words when the model is sure and many when it isn't. In the widget, top-p 0.5 keeps four
words, *floor*, *bed*, *couch*, and *ground*, whose chances add up to 58%.

<At levels="deep">

Written down, top-p keeps the smallest set $V$ of most likely tokens with
$\sum_{i \in V} p_i \ge p$, then divides each kept chance by that sum so they add up to 1.

</At>

## The same prompt, three ways

Here is GPT-2 continuing the same prompt for 16 more pieces of text, at three temperatures:

<RecordedRuns />

At temperature 0 the text is sensible but predictable, and run again on the same computer it comes
out the same every time. At 0.7 it varies but stays readable. At 1.5 it drifts into nonsense,
because unlikely words keep getting picked and each one makes the next guess harder.

<At levels="deep">

### Temperature 0 on a busy server

On a hosted model, temperature 0 isn't always repeatable. When researchers asked Qwen3-235B for the
same 1,000-token answer 1,000 times at temperature 0, they got 80 different answers, all identical
for the first 102 tokens. The server groups many people's requests into one batch, and the size of
the batch changes the order in which the model's numbers are added up. With rounding, a different
order gives slightly different scores, so when two tokens are almost tied, a different one can come
out on top.<Cite id="he2025nondeterminism" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A model outputs a probability distribution; sampling picks the actual word.
- Temperature sharpens (below 1) or flattens (above 1) the distribution. Temperature 0 always picks
  the top word.
- Top-k only allows the k most likely words.
- Top-p only allows the most likely words until their chances add up to p.

<At levels="deep">

- $p_i = e^{z_i / T} / \sum_j e^{z_j / T}$: temperature divides the raw scores before softmax.

</At>
</RecapCard>

